#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# moebius_mu

#% text
# `moebius_mu(n)` gives the Moebius function: 0 when a square of a prime divides `n`, otherwise `(-1)^k` with `k` the number of prime factors of `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `moebius_mu(1)` is 1. The function is multiplicative.

#% item
# `moebius_mu` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
moebius_mu(30)
#% output
#= -1

#% cell
moebius_mu(12)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
moebius_mu(7)
#% output
#= -1

#% cell
moebius_mu(range(12))
#% output
#= [1, -1, -1, 0, -1, 1, -1, 0, 0, 1, -1, 0]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The Mertens function, the partial sum of `moebius_mu`, up to 1000:

#% cell
sum(moebius_mu(range(1000)))
#% output
#= 2

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The sum over the divisors is 0 except for `n = 1`:

#% cell
table(divisor_sum(n, moebius_mu), n, 6)
#% output
#= [1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]

#% text
# Dirichlet inversion of `zeta`: the sum of `moebius_mu(n)/n^2` is `1/zeta(2)`:

#% cell
[sum(moebius_mu(range(20000))/range(20000)^2.0), 6/pi^2.0]
#% output
#= [0.6079271711101355, 0.6079271018540267]

